so the question is will donald trump be  able to maintain all of the benefits of  being a president when he leaves office  things like a pension  things like a secret service detachment  to protect him well the answer  is maybe but probably a lot of this is  defined by the former president's act  which  defines a former president as someone  who shall have held the office of  president of the united states of  america and  whose service in such office shall have  been terminated other than by removal  pursuant to section 4 of article 2 of  the constitution so in other words you  get all the benefits of the former  president's act so long as you weren't  removed pursuant to a trial  after impeachment so it seems like if  donald trump resigned he'd still get the  benefits of the former president's acts  and he'd get all of those benefits so  long as he isn't removed  pursuant to the senate trial of  impeachment and as i've covered before  that seems incredibly unlikely fellow  legal commentator richard hogue has done  a whole video about it and he goes into  great detail about the former  president's act i recommend you check it  out  and as always i'll see you in court
 

